  "live_records": {
    "q-81234": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81234",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/5df807dc424366c043211f7d3a1667bc2c9888f24292856ed99a07d7ac586a94.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=3-(y-3)^{2},\\,$ $x=(y-3)^{2}+4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/3191f16e4c72739688fa4243b44a374a027b307b6e4d419217545e924e1c7a4a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,3-(y-3)^{2}\\le x\\le (y-3)^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81234-a",
