    "q-132406": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132406",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/8918eaed27d1751dbe43c0eeed2a3fc608e3c1254011216e872b32159f570fc3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt[3]{y}}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132406-a",
