    "q-81294": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81294",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/79bbf4ae3d1a28eee2cbbbadc72dde1098bbd3dce42390feaeab591e162dfee6.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=y,\\,$ $x=2-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{xy}\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y+2y-2 & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)+2(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/4eae7bc6756ef5df750bc98431f9e20c9c23869d5ede7f623e79bf7cbb1785c1.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{xy}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{xy}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{xy}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
