    "q-132518": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132518",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the line $y=-2x$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{3-x},$ above the line $y=0.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/3e14010eb6d16c2011b3e97f0fbb81e92035353b214dd79a94be77ffd6f811a9.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=-2x$ and $y=\\sqrt{3-x}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{3-x} & \\,⟹\\,x=3-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{2} & =3-{y}^{2} \\\\ -y & =6-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-y-6 & =0 \\\\ (2y+3)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/6114746f7da893150e0f140395292a3786e8332ea0931e9f77d81c413e558ef0.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 3-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{3-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
