    "q-132512": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132512",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/d90e750755a9520328e99583bfc80d9498ed34eb1695ba4ecd55b688d5b0216f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{2}$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/eeadcddee3bc966372613ded9304c723768e9b81cd0299ad14084a94c9348463.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132512-a",
              "type": "math",
