    "q-81301": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81301",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/c3dbd6e26cb67b2f247387c30e03296f790c3f56c03779a7dda5ae5d0b081958.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=3-{y}^{3},\\,$ $x={y}^{3},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{y}^{3} & ={y}^{3} \\\\ 2{y}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261987/assets/366146b42d476b8d2a467e607a3bba1ef65ddc8a344b3f0e17ee4652ccc0463a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}},\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le 3-{y}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81301-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{3-{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy",
